                "senses": [
                    {
                        "sense_id": "f2f8ebe4-f11f-36c0-ad98-c93838b57937",
                        "entry_id": "801c4675-0848-311a-9687-ee684435d15e",
                        "glosses": [
                            "A type of foot plough with a crooked spade component, used in the Scottish Highlands."
                        ],
                        "tags": [
                            "Scotland"
                        ],
                        "examples": [
                            {
                                "example_id": "d7c9bc19-be11-43b7-a70b-a60570c5274f",
                                "sense_id": "f2f8ebe4-f11f-36c0-ad98-c93838b57937",
                                "text": "1843, James Logan, The Scottish Gaël; Or, Celtic Manners: As Preserved Among the Highlanders, 1st American Edition, page 309,\nThat excellent instrument the cascrom, literally crooked foot, a kind of foot plough, which the Highlanders can manage with great dexterity, and which is too little known,* is still used in mountainous districts, and, from its excellent adaptation to the culture of rugged and steep hills, where a plough cannot be used, is not likely ever to be superseded by any implement."
                            },
                            {
                                "example_id": "561d5db9-54a1-4543-82d1-524fbb61fe98",
                                "sense_id": "f2f8ebe4-f11f-36c0-ad98-c93838b57937",
                                "text": "1866, The St. James's Magazine and United Empire Review, Volume 16: April—July, page 90,\nThe other was the “cascrom,” a very old Celtic implement, intermediate between the spade and the plough."
                            },
                            {
                                "example_id": "91d01336-fb0e-4cf2-a98a-7378aad4ba14",
                                "sense_id": "f2f8ebe4-f11f-36c0-ad98-c93838b57937",
                                "text": "And so complete was the carnage within the gloom of the corrie that never a man of the erewhile friendly crofters returned to work the cascrom within the township of Achnashee."
                            },
                            {
                                "example_id": "4bd34638-afbb-42c9-8557-a3450580a580",
                                "sense_id": "f2f8ebe4-f11f-36c0-ad98-c93838b57937",
                                "text": "The cascrom, or foot-plough, was at this time unknown in Arran."
                            },
                            {
                                "example_id": "25ec870f-be1a-4369-a0e5-c37d42755d95",
                                "sense_id": "f2f8ebe4-f11f-36c0-ad98-c93838b57937",
                                "text": "The ‘cascrom’, or crooked spade, was the most important tool for agriculture throughout the Western Isles and major parts of the mainland Highlands."
                            }
                        ]
                    }
                ],
